      "id": "fb-001",
      "question": "What happens when a SIM goes offline?",
      "shape": "document",
      "guard": "fix",
      "blocked_on": null,
      "asked_as": "1a1db65e-52d5-493b-a1a9-7e495005530a",
      "http_status": 200,
      "seconds": 35.53,
      "answer": "When a SIM goes offline, it can lead to several challenges in fleet management and connectivity. The system is designed to address these challenges by providing features such as:\n\n1. Linking each SIM to a specific tracker, asset, branch, and department to avoid confusion and speed up troubleshooting.\n2. Providing data-spend summaries by telco, country, product, and customer account to monitor connectivity expenses.\n3. Ingesting SIM data automatically from telco APIs to improve accuracy and efficiency.\n4. Offering guided SIM-commissioning steps to ensure proper installation and reduce repeat visits.\n5. Displaying roaming status and estimated cost impact when a unit crosses a border to avoid unexpected bills.\n6. Recommending suitable roaming or local bundles to keep units online at lower costs.\n7. Showing bundle remaining percentages and projected depletion dates to intervene before a unit goes offline.\n8. Displaying network type, signal strength, and recent connectivity gaps for wearable and smartphone SIMs to assess staff reachability.\n\nThese features aim to minimize downtime and improve operational efficiency.\n\nSource: OLIWA User Stories — this is the documented design; it doesn't confirm which parts are live.",
